  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nWinds will be out of the southwest today as our area is on the \nbackside of an expansive high pressure system centered over the \nNortheastern US. As WAA ramps up throughout the day, high \ntemperatures will be in the upper 70s to low 80s with a noticeable \nincrease in humidity compared to the past few days as dew points \nhover around 60 to 65 degrees by the afternoon. Mid level\nstratus clouds are expected to build in later today. Also, skies\nmay appear hazy at times as smoke from ongoing wildfires in the\nPacific Northwest drifts in but remains elevated. No impacts to\nair quality or visibility are expected. In coordination with \nneighboring offices to our west, have trended temperatures and \ndew points down a degree or two due to increased confidence in \npartly to mostly cloudy skies during peak diurnal heating.\n\nHot and humid conditions on Sunday and Monday with potential for \nsevere weather. West/southwest winds will advect in higher dewpoints \naround 70 to 75 degrees by Sunday afternoon and again on Monday. \nLocally higher dewpoints will be possible due to increased \nevapotranspiration from crops. Confidence continues to increase in \nthe 'ring of fire' pattern setting up where our forecast area will \nbe on the periphery of a building upper level ridge centered over \nthe Central Plains. With northwest steering flow aloft, one or more \nMCSs could develop and impact our forecast area late Sunday through \nearly Tuesday. The 'ring of fire' pattern often has a high ceiling \nfor repeated rounds of severe weather but it is also highly \nconditional. There is a wide spread of solutions within model \nguidance which lends itself to considerable uncertainty still. The \nhot and humid conditions will fuel a potentially volatile \nenvironment for storms with up to 3000-4000 J/kg SBCAPE and 40 \nkts of shear. Convective initiation remains uncertain as to \nexactly when and where it will occur Sunday evening; the daytime\nhours will be mainly dry, with the chances for strong to severe\nstorms anticipated to increase after 8 PM EDT lasting through \nabout 4 AM EDT Monday. The greatest uncertainties at this time \nare the potential for capping and what the forcing mechanism \nwill be for convective initiation. As mentioned in the previous \ndiscussion, determining if the catalyst for storms will be \nupper level vorticity maxima, the upper level jet, or remnant \noutflow boundaries/MCVs will be vital in understanding when and \nwhere storms will track. Given this uncertainty, a broad \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe weather encompasses our \nforecast area Sunday night. what happens late Sunday will \ninfluence what occurs on Monday; have maintained the best \nchances for rain/storms on Monday in the evening and early \novernight hours, although this could change. Storms are expected\nto grow upscale into a squall line or mature MCS, with damaging\nwinds as the main threat. A brief embedded tornado or two will \nbe possible as well as 0-1 SRH values approach 150-200 m2/s2 \nSunday night and again on Monday. \n\nExactly how hot and humid it will get on Monday will be determined \nby storms Sunday night. Have maintained highs in the upper 80s to \nlow 90s for now, but diurnal heating may be disrupted or stunted by \nlingering clouds on Monday depending on what happens Sunday. \nMonday's heat indices are around 100 degrees in the forecast, but \nwill hold off on heat headlines for now until better confidence \narises in what will happen Sunday night. \n\nTuesday and beyond will see a reprieve in the active weather pattern \nas high pressure builds over the Great Lakes. Dry and seasonable \nwith highs in the mid 80s midweek. As the upper level ridge builds \nand expands eastward, hot and humid conditions may return by the end \nof next week.",
